    ]
   },
   "cond": {
    "path": "Status",
    "op": "none_eq",
    "value": "Inactive"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x_basic_exec",
   "kind": "attr",
   "tier": "extra",
   "select": "role",
   "cond": {
    "path": "ManagedPolicyArns.*",
    "op": "contains",
    "value": "AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x_python",
   "kind": "attr",
   "tier": "extra",
   "select": "fn",
   "cond": {
    "path": "Runtime",
    "op": "regex",
    "value": "^python"
   }
  }
 ],
 "notes": "SecureString storage is done inside the Lambda code in the GT (no AWS::SSM::Parameter), so the check accepts either an SSM::Parameter resource of Type SecureString or the string in the function code. Custom resource type is matched via __declared__ because Custom::* type names are free and only __declared__ exposes the type as a value; gated copies do not count. The parameter names themselves are built in Lambda code, so names_from_username checks that the username reaches the writer rather than the final names. Inline Lambda code is assumed for conversion_algorithm. The SES policy must be attached to the IAM user or group; the 'limiting' claim is core and rejects extra actions. Review 2026-11-10: lambda_role now requires a role trusting lambda.amazonaws.com rather than any role; policy checks also accept AWS::IAM::UserPolicy, GroupPolicy and RolePolicy resources."
}
